  "content": {
    "id": "p26-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "A general quadratic polynomial $P(x)=ax^2+bx+c$ can always be written as a translation of a quadratic of the form $Q(x)=ax^2$ that is symmetric about the $y$-axis.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p26-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-a-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Show that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-a-identity",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)=Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right).",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-axis-note",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The axis of symmetry of the original quadratic is ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "x=-\\frac{b}{2a}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)=ax^2+bx+c=P(x)$.\n\nExpand the translated quadratic and cancel the constant terms.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)&=a\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2+a\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)^2+b\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+c\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)&=ax^2+bx+\\frac{b^2}{4a}+\\frac{b^2}{4a}-\\frac{b^2}{2a}+c\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)&=ax^2+bx+c\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{2a}\\right)&=P(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      },
      {
        "id": "p26-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-cubic-definition",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "In contrast, a general cubic ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": " cannot always be written as a translation of ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "Q(x)=ax^3",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ". However, it can always be written as a translation of a cubic of the form",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-cubic-formula",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "Q(x)=ax^3+\\left(c-\\frac{b^2}{3a}\\right)x.",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-cubic-symmetry",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "This cubic is symmetric about the origin, since it is an odd function.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-b-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Show that",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-b-identity",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "center",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "P(x)=Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right).",
                  "display": true
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p26-q8-inflection-note",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 1,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Note. The point of symmetry of the original cubic has coordinates ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a},P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)\\right)",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ", and this is the point of inflection of the cubic.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=P(x)$.\n\nExpand both substituted polynomials and collect like terms.",
          "worked": "First evaluate the vertical translation:\n$$\\begin{align*}P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=a\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)^3+b\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)^2+c\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+d\\\\P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=\\frac{2b^3}{27a^2}-\\frac{bc}{3a}+d.\\end{align*}$$\nThen expand the translated cubic:\n$$\\begin{align*}Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=a\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)^3+\\left(c-\\frac{b^2}{3a}\\right)\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=ax^3+bx^2+\\frac{b^2x}{3a}+\\frac{b^3}{27a^2}+cx-\\frac{b^2x}{3a}+\\frac{bc}{3a}-\\frac{b^3}{9a^2}\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=ax^3+bx^2+cx+\\frac{bc}{3a}-\\frac{2b^3}{27a^2}.\\end{align*}$$\nAdding gives\n$$\\begin{align*}Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=ax^3+bx^2+cx+d\\\\Q\\left(x+\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)+P\\left(-\\frac{b}{3a}\\right)&=P(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 85
      }
    ]
